{
  "id": "6ig7jai",
  "title": "Chapter 29: Structure of the Earth",
  "order": 30,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xuhw089",
      "content": "The Earth is made up of several concentric layers that differ in composition, density, temperature and physical state. Knowledge about the internal structure of the Earth is important for understanding earthquakes, volcanoes, plate tectonics and the formation of landforms."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Average Earth Radius",
          "value": "6,371 km",
          "icon": "globe"
        },
        {
          "label": "Major Earth Layers",
          "value": "3 Layers",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Seismic Discontinuities",
          "value": "5 Boundaries",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "cc8q5hz"
    },
    {
      "id": "cnfotuc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Radius and General Facts about the Earth"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Earth's interior is structured into three concentric layers: Crust, Mantle, and Core.",
        "The Crust is divided into thicker, less dense Continental Crust (SIAL) and thinner, denser Oceanic Crust (SIMA).",
        "The Mantle contains the Asthenosphere, a semi-fluid zone responsible for tectonic plate movements.",
        "The Core consists of a liquid Outer Core and a solid Inner Core composed primarily of Nickel and Iron (NIFE).",
        "Seismic discontinuities mark abrupt changes in density and seismic wave velocity between layers."
      ],
      "id": "rest9ke"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3dn256i",
      "content": "• Average radius of Earth: about 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lj49dr1",
      "content": "• Earth's interior temperature increases with depth (geothermal gradient)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l3468jz",
      "content": "• Density also increases toward the center."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g6u8gbm",
      "content": "• The Earth is composed mainly of iron, oxygen, silicon, magnesium and nickel."
    },
    {
      "id": "8kpv8e3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Sources of Information about the Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bg069h2",
      "content": "A. Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8ca676c",
      "content": "• Mining and deep drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hdmvq14",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions bring materials from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "1b1l0tt",
      "content": "B. Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f682nfz",
      "content": "• Seismic waves produced by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h4ounl6",
      "content": "• Gravitational force studies."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "adzaq9q",
      "content": "• Magnetic field observations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bd11rjk",
      "content": "• Meteorite composition which is similar to Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "fs1fegr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Major Layers of the Earth"
    },
    {
