          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "yenpw62",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Crust"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x9m274m",
      "content": "• The crust is the outermost layer of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2mo0zi9",
      "content": "• It is very thin compared to other layers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ajgotax",
      "content": "• Average thickness is about 35 km under continents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "75qt1am",
      "content": "• Oceanic crust is thinner (5–10 km)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "86ilyeb",
      "content": "• Main elements are oxygen, silicon, aluminum, iron and calcium."
    },
    {
      "id": "bi5m947",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Crust"
    },
    {
      "id": "6yuxrbk",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Thickness</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Continental Crust</p>",
            "<p>30–70 km</p>",
            "<p>Granite rocks rich in silica and alumina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oceanic Crust</p>",
            "<p>5–10 km</p>",
            "<p>Basalt rocks rich in silica and magnesium</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3pkfqgp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "txo5ntl",
      "content": "• The mantle lies below the crust and extends up to about 2900 km depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gmfnhrb",
      "content": "• It forms nearly 84% of Earth's volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Continental Crust",
        "Oceanic Crust"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Primary Composition",
          "SIAL (Silica & Aluminium)",
          "SIMA (Silica & Magnesium)"
        ],
        [
          "Dominant Rock Type",
          "Granitic Rocks",
          "Basaltic Rocks"
        ],
        [
          "Density & Thickness",
          "Thicker and Less Dense",
          "Thinner and More Dense"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "xeg8fs8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g41ctj8",
      "content": "• The upper part of the mantle with the crust forms the lithosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qwsjgil",
      "content": "• Below it lies the asthenosphere which contains partially molten rocks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ytbjuyz",
      "content": "• The mantle plays a major role in plate tectonic movements."
    },
    {
      "id": "zqdve8v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Zones in the Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jskbxck",
      "content": "• Lithosphere – rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l8v1sts",
      "content": "• Asthenosphere – semi-molten layer where tectonic plates float."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8ffprly",
      "content": "• Mesosphere – lower mantle with higher density."
    },
    {
      "id": "lmspmr5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ervufgp",
      "content": "• The core is the innermost part of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jarv2dp",
      "content": "• It extends from 2900 km to the center at 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pisswi1",
      "content": "• It is mainly composed of iron and nickel."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g17kl3a",
      "content": "• The core is responsible for generating Earth's magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "c73lyj5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Mohorovičić (Moho) Discontinuity separates the Earth's Crust from the Mantle.",
        "Gutenberg Discontinuity forms the boundary between the Mantle and the Outer Core.",
        "The Outer Core is liquid due to lower pressure, while the Inner Core is solid under intense pressure.",
        "Continental crust is rich in SIAL (Silica and Aluminium), whereas Oceanic crust is rich in SIMA (Silica and Magnesium)."
      ],
      "id": "md2145m"
    },
    {
